WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.700
While he was playfully telling DC's superheroes&nbsp;
to watch their backs as a marketing ploy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:11.460
it seems Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson was&nbsp;
really attempting a DC takeover behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.880
the scenes. Here's how that ploy hit rock bottom.

00:00:15.401 --> 00:00:17.551
Remember when Johnson said this?

00:00:17.551 --> 00:00:22.401
"The hierarchy of power in the&nbsp;
DC Universe is about to change."

00:00:22.401 --> 00:00:27.900
He used this line multiple times in the leadup&nbsp;
to the release of "Black Adam," his DC Films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.240
debut as an ancient, superpowered antihero. Since&nbsp;
then, those words have become meme fodder because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.920
they were right, but not in the way he expected.&nbsp;
The film failed to live up to expectations both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:43.140
critically and financially, and Johnson's plans&nbsp;
for a future with Warner Bros. Discovery crumbled.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.160
In the fall of 2022, Johnson seemed&nbsp;
to be positioning himself not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.660
as DC's latest star, but also as one&nbsp;
of its prime architects. He muscled a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.320
cameo from Henry Cavill as Superman&nbsp;
into "Black Adam." He flooded his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:59.160
social media with videos of himself&nbsp;
in "Black Adam" boardroom meetings.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:04.260
It seems he was really making a clumsy&nbsp;
Machiavellian play to become a DC Films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:09.060
bigwig. According to a bombshell report&nbsp;
from Variety, Johnson and his associates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:13.020
including producers Hiram Garcia,&nbsp;
the A-lister's former brother-in-law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:18.540
and Beau Flynn aimed to put themselves at the&nbsp;
center of future plans for DC Films. Johnson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.820
directly pitched new Warner Bros Discovery&nbsp;
CEO David Zaslav on a "Black Adam" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:29.580
which the star envisioned as a series of movies&nbsp;
leading to a showdown between Adam and Superman.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:36.000
The move reportedly came as former DC studio head&nbsp;
Walter Hamada and WB exec Toby Emmerich were on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.980
their way out on the heels of post-merger&nbsp;
restructuring. The timing did little to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.960
ingratiate Johnson to others in the company.&nbsp;
Variety quotes one unnamed source as saying,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.760
Incoming film chiefs Michael De Luca&nbsp;
and Pam Abdy rubber-stamped Johnson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.140
request for Cavill's Superman cameo in "Black&nbsp;
Adam," but that created a whole other issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:02.340
because Emmerich had decided Cavill's&nbsp;
run as Supes was over in 2018.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.140
Johnson's gambit might have succeeded had&nbsp;
"Black Adam" been the success he promised.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:12.900
But its initial budget of $195 million&nbsp;
ballooned with $40 million in reshoots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.460
and the marketing and other associated costs&nbsp;
were another pretty penny. When the flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:23.940
bagged only $391 million at the worldwide box&nbsp;
office, the possibility of a sequel evaporated.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:29.400
It didn't help that people inside Warner Bros.&nbsp;
Discovery were growing tired of Johnson, whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.500
demands were reportedly becoming increasingly&nbsp;
intolerable. For example, he allegedly insisted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:40.020
on being given a producer credit for "DC League&nbsp;
of Super-Pets," an animated film in which Johnson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:44.160
voices Krypto the Super-Dog. But then he failed&nbsp;
to throw his marketing weight behind the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.440
with anything close to the zeal he reserved for&nbsp;
"Black Adam," leaving it to languish with a $23&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.660
million opening weekend. When "Black Adam"&nbsp;
itself arrived, he demanded a bar featuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:59.586
his personal Teremana brand of tequila at the New&nbsp;
York premiere. As one source put it to Variety,

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.200
Johnson was quickly becoming a&nbsp;
liability the studio couldn't stomach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.020
and DC couldn't make the numbers work anymore.

00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:14.167
"It's been a while since anyone&nbsp;
made the world this nervous."

00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:18.180
Cavill's Superman cameo in the "Black&nbsp;
Adam" post-credits scene is allegedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.380
the result of Johnson's backroom dealings&nbsp;
with CEO David Zaslav. But by the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:29.400
the film opened on October 26, 2022, the,&nbsp;
uh, hierarchy had changed. The day before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:35.280
Warner Bros. Discovery had named James Gunn&nbsp;
and Peter Safran as the new heads of DC Films.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:41.640
Gunn and Safran planned for a soft reset of the&nbsp;
whole franchise, and "Black Adam" bombing only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:45.840
confirmed to them that neither Cavill nor&nbsp;
Johnson should continue in their respective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:51.600
roles. Backed by WB's top players, the pair had&nbsp;
extensive decision-making power to do with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.820
franchise as they pleased. In December, they&nbsp;
finally cut Cavill loose. Later that month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:01.620
Johnson confirmed that he wouldn't be&nbsp;
returning as Black Adam any time soon either.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:07.020
The future still looks murky for DC Films,&nbsp;
though Gunn and Safran seem to have mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:12.360
cleared the board of unwanted pieces, leaving&nbsp;
them free to build a whole new hierarchy.

